          "Caused by wind blowing over rough ground, buildings, trees and hills, producing eddies in the friction layer (lowest 2 000–3 000 ft). It increases with wind strength, surface roughness and instability. Near hangars and buildings it is strong close to the runway. On the lee side of hills it can produce powerful downdraughts and rotors.",
          "§ 3. Frontal turbulence",
          "Caused by the lifting of warm air at a front and by the windshear across the frontal surface. It is most pronounced at fast-moving, active cold fronts with CB, and weak at warm fronts unless CB are embedded.",
