          "A vertical windshear of 6 kt per 1 000 ft or a horizontal shear of 40 kt per 150 NM is typically associated with CAT (rules of thumb). It is usually found in patches about 100 NM long, 20 NM wide and 2 000 ft deep, elongated along the wind. It is not visible and hard to forecast; it is shown on SIGWX charts as dashed areas, and reported in SIGMETs.",
          "Avoidance: change altitude (normally by 2 000–4 000 ft) or move away from the jet core. A change of temperature along the track of more than 5°C in a short distance is a warning sign. Fly at the turbulence penetration speed.",
          "§ Mountain waves (standing or lee waves)",
