          "Hazards: strong updraughts and downdraughts (up to 2 000–5 000 ft/min), with severe downdraughts on the lee slopes that can carry an aircraft into the ground. Rotors, beneath the wave crests, are the most dangerous part: severe turbulence near or below the ridge-top height. Altimeter errors (the altimeter can over-read by up to about 1 000 ft in the strong winds and pressure changes). CAT at high levels.",
          "Visual signs of mountain waves: LENTICULAR clouds (altocumulus or cirrocumulus lenticularis), smooth, lens-shaped and stationary, in the wave crests, sometimes stacked ('pile of plates'). ROTOR (roll) clouds, ragged cumulus-like clouds rotating beneath the crests at or below ridge-top level. CAP cloud (a Föhn wall) on the summit, with a cloud-free lee side. Remember, waves also occur without cloud when the air is dry.",
          "Flying near mountains in strong winds: cross ridges at an angle of 45° so that you can turn away from a downdraught; clear the ridge by at least 2 000 ft (more in strong winds), in strong winds by up to 3 000–5 000 ft; approach on the upwind side where possible; avoid the lee side at low level and the rotor zone. The Drakensberg and the Cape fold mountains produce strong waves in winter north-westerlies ahead of cold fronts.",
