          "§ How fog forms in general",
          "Fog is cloud on the ground. It forms when air near the surface is cooled to its dew point (by radiation, advection over a cold surface, or orographic lifting), or when moisture is added (by evaporation from warm water or rain) until it saturates. It is reported as FG when visibility is less than 1 000 m.",
          "§ Radiation fog",
          "Formation: on a clear night, the ground loses heat by long-wave radiation and cools the air in contact with it by conduction until the air reaches its dew point.",
          "Conditions favouring it: clear skies (allowing strong radiation cooling); light winds of about 2–8 kt, which mix the cooled layer to a depth of a few hundred feet (in calm conditions only dew or frost forms, or very shallow ground fog; in stronger winds, above about 8–10 kt, mixing is too deep and low stratus forms instead); high relative humidity and a small temperature/dew-point spread at dusk (moist air); long nights (autumn and winter); land surfaces, especially low-lying, damp ground, valleys and river beds where cold air drains (katabatic). It typically occurs in anticyclones and cols.",
          "Time: it forms during the night, thickening around and just after dawn (lowest temperature, and the first warming may stir it up and make it thicker), and is normally thickest shortly after sunrise.",
          "Dispersal: the sun warms the ground, which warms the air from below so that the fog evaporates from the bottom, lifting first into low stratus and then breaking up, usually by mid-morning. It will also clear if the wind strengthens (turning it into stratus) or if cloud covers the sky (reducing radiation cooling). In winter, under a strong inversion with a weak sun, it may persist all day. It does NOT form over the sea, because the sea's temperature barely changes overnight.",
          "Radiation fog is common on the Highveld in winter (May to August) at airfields such as FAOR, Lanseria and Bloemfontein, especially after rain has moistened the ground.",
