          "Sea fog: warm, moist air over cold ocean currents. Typical example: the cold BENGUELA current off the west coast of South Africa and Namibia. Air over the current forms sea fog and low stratus, which is carried onshore by the sea breeze and the south-westerly wind, affecting Cape Town, Saldanha, Alexander Bay and Walvis Bay, especially in summer and autumn, often in the mornings. Coastal fog is also common along the south and east coasts when warm air flows over cooler water (e.g. ahead of coastal lows).",
          "§ Frontal fog",
          "Frontal fog forms ahead of a warm front (and at occlusions) when warm rain falls through the cold air beneath the frontal surface and evaporates, adding moisture until the cold air becomes saturated. It may also form when the frontal cloud base lowers to the surface (along the front itself, where the two air masses mix). It can cover a wide area, and comes with low cloud, drizzle and rain.",
          "§ Orographic (hill or upslope) fog",
          "Orographic fog forms when moist, stable air is forced up a slope, cools adiabatically to its dew point, and forms cloud on the high ground. To an observer on the hill it is fog. It can persist as long as the wind continues to blow up the slope. It is common on the slopes of the escarpment and mountains, such as the Drakensberg and the Table Mountain 'tablecloth' with the south-easter.",
          "§ Steam fog (Arctic sea smoke)",
          "Steam fog forms when very cold, dry air moves over much WARMER water. Water evaporates from the warm surface into the cold air, which quickly becomes saturated, and the excess condenses into fog. The fog appears as wisps rising like steam. Since the cold air is heated from below, it is unstable, so the fog is usually shallow and patchy. It occurs over lakes, rivers and dams on cold mornings, and over Arctic and Antarctic seas.",
          "§ Other types",
